WEBVTT

00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:06.600
Welcome to Day 1: The Legal EA and PA Role, Communication and Managing Up.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.125
We'll start with EA versus PA work and the legal basics,

00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:13.875
then move through communication and gatekeeping,

00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:19.075
confidentiality and boundaries, and the command hierarchy and liaison work.

00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:22.925
We finish with managing up, client profiling and the dossier,

00:00:22.925 --> 00:00:25.600
and two frameworks for written communication.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.825
By the end of today, you'll have a clear picture of what a Legal Executive Assistant

00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:35.500
actually does day to day, know when to escalate rather than act alone,

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.175
and be ready to manage up with the Three C's.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.838
Six areas cover most of what a Legal Executive Assistant handles day to day:

00:00:44.838 --> 00:00:48.412
calendar and scheduling, correspondence and email,

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:52.312
document and case support, court and filing coordination,

00:00:52.312 --> 00:00:56.500
billing and client support, and confidentiality and compliance.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.625
Each one comes with real tasks and a sample from Elias's world,

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:05.062
like blocking two hours of prep before every deposition,

00:01:05.062 --> 00:01:09.412
or flagging a client's retainer once it drops below 25 percent.

00:01:09.412 --> 00:01:14.350
Get these right, and his court dates, deadlines and clients stay protected.

00:01:16.325 --> 00:01:20.475
In the live class, your trainer steps into the role of Elias Thorne for a few

00:01:20.475 --> 00:01:24.225
minutes, before you open a single document about him.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.212
Here, think about what you'd ask him on your actual first day.

00:01:28.212 --> 00:01:32.488
How does he prefer to communicate, and how will you know if you've gotten it wrong?

00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:35.775
What's the one thing that would lose his trust fastest?

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.300
Hold on to those questions.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.150
You'll build the real dossier right after this.

00:01:43.075 --> 00:01:46.675
Section 1 of 8: EA versus PA Roles.

00:01:46.675 --> 00:01:50.950
We'll look at the two mindsets, how the work context differs side by side,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.525
and the decision principles behind each.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.300
Before you touch any request, you need to know which hat you're wearing.

00:02:00.075 --> 00:02:03.825
As an EA, you're looking after the business: your boss's time,

00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:06.400
his decisions and the firm's results.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.925
As a PA, you're looking after the person: his family,

00:02:09.925 --> 00:02:13.162
his errands, the life that keeps him able to work.

00:02:13.162 --> 00:02:16.988
The trouble starts when we answer a business problem with errand energy,

00:02:16.988 --> 00:02:19.950
or treat a family matter like a board meeting.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.362
So first, when something lands, we ask one quiet question:

00:02:24.362 --> 00:02:26.975
am I protecting the business, or the person?

00:02:26.975 --> 00:02:29.075
Next, we check the dossier.

00:02:29.075 --> 00:02:31.400
If the answer's there, we use it.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:38.325
Then, if it's honestly unclear, we play it safe and treat it as business work until

00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:39.862
we can confirm.

00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:44.475
Handling something important like a small errand is the costlier mistake.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.362
Once we've picked, we commit.

00:02:48.362 --> 00:02:51.400
Business work gets a careful, by-the-book response;

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.250
personal work gets a warm, flexible one.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:59.288
And finally, if we got it wrong, we say so, fix it and remember the example.

00:02:59.288 --> 00:03:01.775
That's how this instinct gets sharper.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.987
Here's a situation.

00:03:03.987 --> 00:03:07.050
A new assistant keeps one to-do list for Elias:

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.162
'Board deck edits, dry cleaning, Meridian call notes,

00:03:10.162 --> 00:03:15.212
flowers for Sarah, expense report.' By Wednesday the dry cleaning is done and the

00:03:15.212 --> 00:03:16.612
board deck isn't.

00:03:16.612 --> 00:03:19.400
What went wrong, and how would you sort that list?

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.300
Both roles earn the same trust.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.300
What changes is the setting.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.250
Picture two calls: a firm partner about a filing,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.937
and Elias's wife about a school event.

00:03:31.937 --> 00:03:34.475
You'd sound different on each, and you should.

00:03:34.475 --> 00:03:38.125
The office runs on structure, policy and formality;

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:42.825
the personal side runs on relationships and the person's own preferences.

00:03:42.825 --> 00:03:45.675
Read which one you're in before you respond.

00:03:46.475 --> 00:03:49.912
First, we remember that each side has its own secrets.

00:03:49.912 --> 00:03:53.600
Office secrets are contracts, money and HR matters;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.000
personal secrets are family, health and lifestyle.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.425
Protecting one doesn't cover the other.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.162
After that, we stay inside our own role.

00:04:04.162 --> 00:04:08.362
As an EA we filter and prioritize on the executive's behalf;

00:04:08.362 --> 00:04:11.600
as a PA we handle day-to-day things directly.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.400
And finally, when the two overlap, like a personal matter with real business risk,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.350
we say so out loud.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:20.362
Try this one.

00:04:20.362 --> 00:04:24.175
Elias texts late at night, 'Book the usual table for Friday,

00:04:24.175 --> 00:04:28.500
and send the Meridian team the updated timeline.' Which half is which,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.200
and how do you handle each?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.537
The same kind of request can deserve two different answers.

00:04:35.537 --> 00:04:37.550
Take a request to move a meeting.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.812
On the business side, we ask whether it matters to money,

00:04:40.812 --> 00:04:44.275
deadlines or legal risk, and protect it if it does.

00:04:44.275 --> 00:04:48.075
On the personal side, we start from what the person prefers.

00:04:48.875 --> 00:04:50.562
Neither answer is wrong.

00:04:50.562 --> 00:04:54.450
What goes wrong is using the wrong set of rules without noticing.

00:04:56.375 --> 00:04:58.550
First, we name which track we're on.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.650
Next, on the business track, important work goes first,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.700
we narrow things down before bothering the executive,

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.925
and we know which calls are ours.

00:05:06.925 --> 00:05:10.550
Then, on the personal track, the person's wishes lead.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.275
We offer options instead of saying no, and we keep private matters private.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.662
After that, if a decision doesn't fit either set of rules, we escalate.

00:05:21.662 --> 00:05:24.000
That's not the moment to trust our gut.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.575
Finally, every so often we look back at our own calls,

00:05:27.575 --> 00:05:30.975
to catch ourselves slipping into one habit for everything.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:36.725
Try this one: Elias wants to move a client call to attend his daughter's recital,

00:05:36.725 --> 00:05:39.375
but that client is mid-negotiation.

00:05:39.375 --> 00:05:42.350
Which rules apply, and what do you actually do?

00:05:44.275 --> 00:05:47.138
The job looks different depending on where you land.

00:05:47.138 --> 00:05:52.100
A corporate office, a law firm, a remote startup or a private family home:

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.238
similar tasks, very different expectations.

00:05:55.238 --> 00:05:59.550
Even guessing wrong about which email system they use can slow down your whole first

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:00.375
week.

00:06:00.375 --> 00:06:04.800
So in any new role, spend the first days learning how this place works.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.075
First, we fit our response to the setting.

00:06:09.075 --> 00:06:13.650
When a vendor lets us down, an EA pushes back or escalates professionally;

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.100
a PA just gets the problem fixed.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.525
Next, clashing meetings: an EA moves things by business priority;

00:06:20.525 --> 00:06:23.250
a PA checks what the person would prefer.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.525
Finally, when a request quietly grows beyond our job,

00:06:26.525 --> 00:06:32.100
an EA points out the contract line; a PA asks permission before going further.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.563
Here's a situation.

00:06:34.563 --> 00:06:38.850
A catering vendor sends the wrong order to a client lunch at the office,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.938
and the same vendor is booked for the Thornes' dinner party on Saturday.

00:06:42.938 --> 00:06:47.850
How does your EA side handle the lunch, and your PA side handle Saturday?

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:52.513
Section 2 of 8: Legal Basics.

00:06:52.513 --> 00:06:55.475
Who's who in a law firm, the life of a legal matter,

00:06:55.475 --> 00:06:57.975
and the legal terms you'll hear every day.

00:06:59.875 --> 00:07:03.825
Before you can route a single request well, you need to know who's who.

00:07:04.625 --> 00:07:07.388
Partners own the firm and the client relationships.

00:07:07.388 --> 00:07:10.875
Associates are lawyers doing much of the day-to-day legal work.

00:07:10.875 --> 00:07:14.738
Of counsel are experienced lawyers with a looser tie to the firm.

00:07:14.738 --> 00:07:17.975
Paralegals do real legal work under a lawyer's supervision,

00:07:17.975 --> 00:07:23.213
and we, the assistants, keep everyone's time, communication and logistics running.

00:07:23.213 --> 00:07:26.400
Outside the firm, there are clients, opposing counsel,

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.575
court clerks, judges' chambers, court reporters and experts.

00:07:32.525 --> 00:07:34.988
First, in week one, we get the org chart.

00:07:34.988 --> 00:07:38.113
Next, for each of Elias's matters, we write down the team:

00:07:38.113 --> 00:07:41.388
partner, associate, paralegal and client contact.

00:07:41.388 --> 00:07:45.488
Then the outside names: opposing counsel, the judge and our vendors.

00:07:45.488 --> 00:07:49.475
After that, before passing anything on, we ask who owns that decision.

00:07:49.475 --> 00:07:51.675
Legal judgment goes to a lawyer.

00:07:51.675 --> 00:07:55.625
Finally, we address people properly: 'Judge Rivera' in writing,

00:07:55.625 --> 00:07:57.250
'Your Honor' in court.

00:07:58.075 --> 00:07:59.438
Now a situation.

00:07:59.438 --> 00:08:02.300
A voicemail says, 'This is Mark from Harlow's side,

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.900
about the deposition.' Before you call back, who is Mark,

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.925
and who at the firm should handle it?

00:08:09.825 --> 00:08:14.375
If you know which stage a matter is in, you can see the next deadline coming.

00:08:15.175 --> 00:08:17.963
Everything a firm works on is called a matter.

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:20.975
A lawsuit starts with intake and a conflict check,

00:08:20.975 --> 00:08:23.388
then an engagement letter and a retainer.

00:08:23.388 --> 00:08:27.438
Then investigation, then pleadings, the complaint and the answer.

00:08:27.438 --> 00:08:32.138
Then discovery, where both sides exchange documents and take depositions.

00:08:32.138 --> 00:08:36.225
Then motions, settlement or trial, judgment and maybe an appeal,

00:08:36.225 --> 00:08:38.325
and finally closing the file.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:43.638
Deals run a shorter path: intake, engagement, drafting and negotiating,

00:08:43.638 --> 00:08:45.450
signing, then the follow-up.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.600
First, when a matter opens, we note whether it's litigation or a deal,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.300
and which stage it's in.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:58.038
Next, at intake, we collect every party's name and every key date.

00:08:58.038 --> 00:09:01.375
Then we calendar each deadline the moment it's triggered.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.300
After that, before any hearing, trial or signing, we build the checklist early.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.550
Finally, at closing: the final bill, any trust money returned,

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:15.225
a closing letter and the file archived.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.850
Here's a quick one to think about: why might a lawyer be alarmed if you said a

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.950
document was 'filed' when it was only 'sent'?

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:29.325
One misunderstood word can send the wrong document to the wrong place.

00:09:30.075 --> 00:09:33.238
The plaintiff brings the case and the defendant answers it;

00:09:33.238 --> 00:09:35.738
someone without a lawyer is 'pro se'.

00:09:35.738 --> 00:09:39.063
A complaint starts a lawsuit, an answer responds,

00:09:39.063 --> 00:09:43.388
a motion asks the court to do something, and a brief argues why.

00:09:43.388 --> 00:09:47.012
An affidavit is a sworn statement signed before a notary;

00:09:47.012 --> 00:09:49.825
a declaration is signed under penalty of perjury,

00:09:49.825 --> 00:09:51.300
without a notary.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.250
In discovery, interrogatories are written questions,

00:09:55.250 --> 00:10:00.325
a deposition is sworn testimony outside court, and a subpoena orders someone to show

00:10:00.325 --> 00:10:02.662
up or hand over documents.

00:10:02.662 --> 00:10:05.850
A stipulation is something both sides agree on.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:12.400
First, we keep a one-page glossary in our own words and add to it every week.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.463
Next, when we hear a term we don't know, we write it down and look it up.

00:10:16.463 --> 00:10:19.238
Then we use each document's exact name.

00:10:19.238 --> 00:10:23.850
After that, we learn the firm's own words, like matter number and retainer.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:27.800
Finally, we notice which side the firm is on in each matter.

00:10:28.625 --> 00:10:29.888
Now try this.

00:10:29.888 --> 00:10:34.725
Elias leaves a voice note: 'Opposing counsel served interrogatories and noticed

00:10:34.725 --> 00:10:40.138
Harlow's CFO for deposition; calendar the responses and get a court reporter.'

00:10:40.138 --> 00:10:43.175
Translate it into a plain-English task list.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.400
Taking on the wrong client can force a firm off a case,

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:52.325
so the first job with any new inquiry is the conflict check.

00:10:53.075 --> 00:10:57.663
It's an ethics rule: the firm can't take on anyone who'd clash with a current or

00:10:57.663 --> 00:10:59.025
former client.

00:10:59.025 --> 00:11:02.100
Until the check clears and an engagement letter is signed,

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:07.375
the caller is a prospective client, so we take only what we need and give no advice.

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:10.725
And a date mentioned in passing at intake matters.

00:11:12.675 --> 00:11:16.225
First, we use the intake form every time: all parties,

00:11:16.225 --> 00:11:21.300
related businesses, opposing counsel, a short description and any dates.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:25.963
Next, we search every name, including former names and parent companies.

00:11:25.963 --> 00:11:28.150
Then any match goes to the attorney.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.825
We don't decide whether it's a real conflict.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.012
After that, any deadline mentioned goes to the attorney the same day.

00:11:37.012 --> 00:11:41.950
Finally, once cleared, we prepare the engagement letter and open the matter only

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.325
after it's signed.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:45.688
Here's a situation.

00:11:45.688 --> 00:11:50.275
A caller wants to sue 'Northgate Logistics' over a warehouse contract that ended

00:11:50.275 --> 00:11:53.900
'almost six years ago.' What do you search, what do you flag,

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.350
and what do you not say?

00:11:57.325 --> 00:12:00.912
Section 3 of 8: Communication and Gatekeeping.

00:12:00.912 --> 00:12:04.075
Why gatekeeping is not 'no', the filtering matrix,

00:12:04.075 --> 00:12:07.050
and scripts that redirect without alienating.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.800
In legal work, a message that can be read two ways is a risk,

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:14.700
not just a style problem.

00:12:15.475 --> 00:12:17.912
A busy attorney wants the facts fast.

00:12:17.912 --> 00:12:21.338
A nervous client wants to understand and feel reassured.

00:12:21.338 --> 00:12:24.325
A court clerk wants it formal and by the book.

00:12:25.125 --> 00:12:28.500
So first, before we write a word, we decide who's reading it.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:32.012
Next, we write it so there's only one way to understand it.

00:12:32.012 --> 00:12:34.238
Then we set the tone for that reader.

00:12:34.238 --> 00:12:38.600
After that, we remember that confidentiality covers conversations too,

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.100
even a casual chat in the hallway.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:45.700
Finally, when we have to say 'not now', we use tried-and-tested phrases,

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:48.525
so we sound professional instead of flustered.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.812
Here's a situation.

00:12:50.812 --> 00:12:55.100
In the lift, a colleague from another team asks, 'Did the Meridian thing settle?

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:59.025
I heard it was big money.' The settlement is confidential.

00:12:59.025 --> 00:13:02.800
What do you say, without sounding rude or confirming anything?

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:05.500
Quick check.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.762
Updating a client on a filing you just made, should the tone be A,

00:13:08.762 --> 00:13:12.600
plain language and reassuring, focused on what it means for them?

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.512
B, skipped, because clients don't need filing updates?

00:13:16.512 --> 00:13:19.600
C, as brief as possible with no context?

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.875
Or D, identical to what you'd send the court clerk?

00:13:23.725 --> 00:13:25.000
Take a second.

00:13:25.775 --> 00:13:28.600
It's A: plain language and reassuring.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:32.800
Reading the audience means the same event gets a different register for the client

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.125
than for the court or the attorney.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.575
Good gatekeeping never slams the door.

00:13:39.575 --> 00:13:42.650
It says 'not right now, but here's what we can do.'

00:13:43.475 --> 00:13:45.938
A flat no makes people go around you.

00:13:45.938 --> 00:13:50.875
A calm, confident 'not now, here's a better option' keeps the relationship and still

00:13:50.875 --> 00:13:53.025
protects the executive's time.

00:13:53.025 --> 00:13:55.725
And when you speak for him, you carry his authority,

00:13:55.725 --> 00:13:58.425
so there's no need to sound apologetic.

00:14:00.375 --> 00:14:03.500
First, we never make the person feel wrong for asking,

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:05.688
or tell them they should have planned better.

00:14:05.688 --> 00:14:07.400
That wins us nothing.

00:14:09.325 --> 00:14:11.962
After that, we always hand them something real:

00:14:11.962 --> 00:14:16.025
a specific time, a named colleague or a clear timeline.

00:14:16.025 --> 00:14:20.812
Finally, if they push back, we listen, explain the real constraint honestly and

00:14:20.812 --> 00:14:25.000
appeal to what we both want, instead of repeating the same no louder.

00:14:25.775 --> 00:14:27.363
Here's a situation.

00:14:27.363 --> 00:14:32.012
A junior partner has asked for time with Elias three times this week and is getting

00:14:32.012 --> 00:14:33.225
frustrated.

00:14:33.225 --> 00:14:35.688
Elias can't meet until next week.

00:14:35.688 --> 00:14:39.500
How does the partner leave feeling heard, with a real next step?

00:14:41.425 --> 00:14:44.488
Not every request deserves the same speed.

00:14:44.488 --> 00:14:48.925
Answer them in the order they arrived, and the important one gets buried under five

00:14:48.925 --> 00:14:50.175
easy ones.

00:14:50.975 --> 00:14:54.625
Instead, we run each request through four questions, in order.

00:14:54.625 --> 00:14:55.800
Does it affect money?

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:57.450
Does it carry legal risk?

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.625
Does it need the executive himself?

00:14:59.625 --> 00:15:01.575
Is there a relationship on the line?

00:15:01.575 --> 00:15:04.475
If any single answer is high, it's urgent.

00:15:04.475 --> 00:15:06.825
One serious reason is enough.

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:13.262
Then, if everything scores low, we decide whether to hand it to someone else,

00:15:13.262 --> 00:15:16.475
schedule it for later or offer another option.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:22.850
After that, we jot down how we sorted it, especially anything we parked,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:25.000
so our thinking can be checked later.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:29.538
Finally, we come back to parked items exactly when we said we would.

00:15:29.538 --> 00:15:32.825
A forgotten item is as bad as a mishandled one.

00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:35.063
Now think about this.

00:15:35.063 --> 00:15:40.575
Elias's oldest client asks, 'Can he sign a birthday card for our CEO today?' It

00:15:40.575 --> 00:15:43.912
scores low on money, legal risk and authority.

00:15:43.912 --> 00:15:46.475
Why might it still go near the top of the list?

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:53.350
Having a few ready-made ways to redirect people means we never have to improvise a

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:54.650
clumsy no.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:59.150
If the request is fine, just badly timed, we defer it.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.800
If it belongs to someone else, we re-route it.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.200
And if there's simply no room, we say so plainly and offer something else.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:08.825
We call that one Drastic Contrast.

00:16:09.625 --> 00:16:13.813
First, when we defer, we give a real timeline, not a vague 'soon'.

00:16:13.813 --> 00:16:18.125
Then, when we re-route, we name the right person and make sure the handoff actually

00:16:18.125 --> 00:16:19.200
happens.

00:16:21.125 --> 00:16:25.413
After that, when we're being plainly honest, we tell the truth about the constraint

00:16:25.413 --> 00:16:28.475
and immediately offer a specific alternative.

00:16:28.475 --> 00:16:32.425
Finally, whichever we use, we try to get a small yes first,

00:16:32.425 --> 00:16:34.450
like confirming what they need.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:36.788
Now a situation.

00:16:36.788 --> 00:16:40.688
You re-routed a client's billing question to finance a week ago.

00:16:40.688 --> 00:16:45.438
Today the client emails Elias directly, annoyed that nobody replied.

00:16:45.438 --> 00:16:48.500
What went wrong with your redirect, and what do you do now?

00:16:50.425 --> 00:16:54.512
For a lot of clients, your voice on the phone is their first impression of the whole

00:16:54.512 --> 00:16:55.400
firm.

00:16:56.175 --> 00:16:59.775
A good message lets the attorney act without calling us back:

00:16:59.775 --> 00:17:02.675
who called, from where, their number, which matter,

00:17:02.675 --> 00:17:06.425
what they need, how urgent it is and when to reach them.

00:17:06.425 --> 00:17:10.725
And we don't confirm that someone is a client until we know who we're talking to.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:15.850
First, answer with the firm's name and your name.

00:17:15.850 --> 00:17:18.975
Next, take the full message, read the number back,

00:17:18.975 --> 00:17:22.050
and ask which matter and whether there's a deadline.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:27.175
Then transfer warmly: tell the attorney who it is and why before connecting.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:32.800
After that, when you leave a voicemail, say your number slowly at the start and the

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:36.650
end, keep it short and leave out anything confidential.

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:41.400
Finally, log important calls and return them within one business day.

00:17:42.175 --> 00:17:43.612
Here's a situation.

00:17:43.612 --> 00:17:45.675
A caller says, 'I'm a reporter.

00:17:45.675 --> 00:17:47.900
Is Harlow Industries one of your clients?

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:51.350
I just need a yes or no.' What exactly do you say,

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:53.225
and what do you do after the call?

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:59.275
Whether you're speaking as an EA or a PA, the instinct is the same:

00:17:59.275 --> 00:18:01.775
protect first, sort it out second.

00:18:02.575 --> 00:18:05.962
Say someone calls asking for confidential information.

00:18:05.962 --> 00:18:10.350
As an EA, we're firm, brief and procedural: we can't release that,

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.450
and we'll escalate it internally.

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:16.250
As a PA, we're warm but just as protective: that's private,

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.488
but we're happy to help with logistics.

00:18:18.488 --> 00:18:20.312
Both keep the door closed.

00:18:20.312 --> 00:18:23.400
One protects the business, the other the person.

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:29.238
First, when something sensitive comes in, we decide in that moment whether we're

00:18:29.238 --> 00:18:32.038
answering as an EA or a PA.

00:18:32.038 --> 00:18:35.887
Then, when someone keeps pushing, we hold the same line.

00:18:35.887 --> 00:18:41.375
The EA asks for it in writing; the PA takes a message and promises a follow-up.

00:18:42.125 --> 00:18:43.637
Here's a situation.

00:18:43.637 --> 00:18:48.075
A caller from a national newspaper asks whether Thorne and Partners is representing

00:18:48.075 --> 00:18:50.912
a well-known CEO in a fraud case.

00:18:50.912 --> 00:18:55.350
Two minutes later, Sarah Thorne's friend calls asking whether Elias and Sarah are

00:18:55.350 --> 00:19:01.050
'going through a rough patch.' Answer the first as an EA and the second as a PA.

00:19:02.925 --> 00:19:03.837
Quick check.

00:19:03.837 --> 00:19:08.738
In the 'Persistent Caller' scenario, does the EA's scripted response focus on A,

00:19:08.738 --> 00:19:10.825
immediately connecting the call?

00:19:10.825 --> 00:19:13.662
B, promising a callback within the hour?

00:19:13.662 --> 00:19:17.000
C, asking for a written request to review priority?

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.975
Or D, ignoring the caller entirely?

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:22.075
Take a second.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:26.725
It's C: asking for a written request to review priority.

00:19:26.725 --> 00:19:30.762
It filters the request into a reviewable, prioritized channel,

00:19:30.762 --> 00:19:34.150
rather than granting or flatly denying access.

00:19:36.075 --> 00:19:39.675
The record of a meeting can't depend on technology alone.

00:19:39.675 --> 00:19:43.625
When the recording fails, or everyone remembers a different decision,

00:19:43.625 --> 00:19:47.075
the assistant is the one people ask what was agreed.

00:19:47.075 --> 00:19:50.350
So the record is our job, not the software's.

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:54.288
First, we test the recording before the meeting starts.

00:19:54.288 --> 00:19:56.250
Halfway through is too late.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:59.850
Next, we always have a person taking notes as a backup.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:03.250
Then, we capture what was decided and who's doing what,

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.650
and leave out the side chatter and off-the-record comments.

00:20:08.575 --> 00:20:13.050
After that, if an AI tool made a transcript, we read it and clean it up before

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:14.600
anyone else sees it.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:18.112
Finally, we give the executive what he actually needs:

00:20:18.112 --> 00:20:22.000
a short briefing memo, not forty pages of transcript.

00:20:22.775 --> 00:20:24.438
Here's a situation.

00:20:24.438 --> 00:20:28.750
The AI transcript of a client call spells the client's company wrong,

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:33.812
records a side comment about opposing counsel and lists an action item nobody agreed

00:20:33.812 --> 00:20:34.688
to.

00:20:34.688 --> 00:20:38.800
What do you fix before anything is shared, and what goes to Elias?

00:20:40.725 --> 00:20:44.425
Section 4 of 8: Confidentiality and Boundaries.

00:20:44.425 --> 00:20:47.525
Professional standards, authorization protocols,

00:20:47.525 --> 00:20:50.100
and what an assistant can and can't do.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:56.900
Everything we see is protected, but business secrets and personal privacy work a

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:58.250
little differently.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:01.500
On the business side, we're guarding contracts,

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:07.150
financial numbers and ideas the firm owns, with real laws and HR rules behind them,

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:09.612
so we know those rules ahead of time.

00:21:09.612 --> 00:21:13.025
On the personal side, we're guarding a family's privacy,

00:21:13.025 --> 00:21:16.000
and what holds that together is trust more than rules,

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.175
so discretion is the default.

00:21:18.975 --> 00:21:23.200
Either way, if we're not sure whether something is confidential, it is.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.462
We treat every channel as risky.

00:21:27.462 --> 00:21:31.550
A chat in the lift can do as much damage as a misdirected email.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:33.712
And when in doubt, we protect it.

00:21:33.712 --> 00:21:38.250
Over-protecting costs almost nothing; a leak can cost everything.

00:21:39.025 --> 00:21:40.663
Here's one to think about.

00:21:40.663 --> 00:21:44.100
In the lobby, a friendly paralegal from another firm says,

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:46.200
'Is Elias on the Meridian matter?

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:50.575
I heard it's getting messy.' What do you say, and what do you avoid saying?

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:57.275
Knowing exactly how far our authority goes is what lets us act quickly without

00:21:57.275 --> 00:21:58.725
getting into trouble.

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:03.550
Every assistant has limits on money, access and commitments.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:08.625
Some are written down; many are just assumed, and the assumed ones cause problems.

00:22:08.625 --> 00:22:12.875
For example: an invoice under 500 dollars, we can approve;

00:22:12.875 --> 00:22:15.925
over that, we escalate first, every time.

00:22:16.725 --> 00:22:21.300
So first, we check anything involving money, access or a commitment against our

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.700
written limits, not our memory.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:27.975
Next, if it's within our limit, we handle it and note that we did.

00:22:29.925 --> 00:22:33.575
Then, if it's over our limit, we escalate before we act.

00:22:33.575 --> 00:22:36.350
'It'll probably be approved' isn't approval.

00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:42.288
After that, we keep business and personal systems completely separate:

00:22:42.288 --> 00:22:45.300
different calendars, storage and devices.

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:49.325
Finally, when something's unclear, we ask: is it within policy,

00:22:49.325 --> 00:22:52.650
is it legal, and would the executive trust us doing it?

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:56.150
If any answer is no or unsure, we escalate.

00:22:56.925 --> 00:22:58.575
Here's a situation.

00:22:58.575 --> 00:23:03.312
Sarah Thorne asks you to book a family flight upgrade that goes 300 dollars over the

00:23:03.312 --> 00:23:05.362
travel budget Elias set.

00:23:05.362 --> 00:23:10.050
She says, 'He won't mind.' What do you do, and how do you say it to her?

00:23:11.975 --> 00:23:15.700
Clients will ask you for legal advice, kindly and often,

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:18.725
and saying yes even once can put the firm at risk.

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:23.125
Only a licensed lawyer can give legal advice, set fees,

00:23:23.125 --> 00:23:26.988
take on a case, sign court papers or appear in court.

00:23:26.988 --> 00:23:31.775
Doing that without a license is the unauthorized practice of law, and it's illegal.

00:23:31.775 --> 00:23:36.112
And because the lawyer supervises us, our slip becomes the firm's problem.

00:23:36.112 --> 00:23:40.338
The line is interpretation: telling someone what the law means for them,

00:23:40.338 --> 00:23:42.675
or what they should do, is advice.

00:23:44.625 --> 00:23:47.788
First, what we can do: schedule, gather documents,

00:23:47.788 --> 00:23:51.750
share public facts like a court's hours, and relay the attorney's advice word for

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:52.675
word.

00:23:52.675 --> 00:23:55.900
Next, what we can't do: say whether someone has a case,

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:59.238
what a document means for them or which option to choose.

00:23:59.238 --> 00:24:03.875
Then, when asked, we use a bridge line: 'That's a great question for Elias.

00:24:03.875 --> 00:24:05.750
I'll make sure he gets it today.'

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:11.725
After that, we quote the attorney exactly, or have them put it in writing.

00:24:11.725 --> 00:24:15.525
Finally, when in doubt, we treat it as advice and route it.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:17.438
Then try this.

00:24:17.438 --> 00:24:21.512
A client calls: 'The other side offered $40,000.

00:24:21.512 --> 00:24:23.662
Elias is in trial all week.

00:24:23.662 --> 00:24:27.675
Just between us, should I take it?' Write exactly what you'd say.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:33.088
A non-disclosure agreement is a real legal promise,

00:24:33.088 --> 00:24:36.200
so we need to know what's in it before we rely on it.

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:41.075
The mistake is treating 'they signed an NDA' as a magic shield.

00:24:41.075 --> 00:24:42.575
Some have expired.

00:24:42.575 --> 00:24:45.400
Some only cover certain kinds of information.

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:48.500
And sometimes nobody actually signed one at all.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:52.450
So first, before sharing anything sensitive with a vendor,

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:57.500
contractor or temp, we check there's an NDA on file for that exact person or

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:58.625
company.

00:24:58.625 --> 00:25:02.462
Next, we check what it covers and whether it's still in date.

00:25:02.462 --> 00:25:07.275
Then, if we can't confirm it covers this request, we pause and verify.

00:25:07.275 --> 00:25:09.775
We don't go ahead on 'it's probably fine'.

00:25:11.725 --> 00:25:14.500
After that, we keep a simple list of who's signed,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:18.100
what it covers and when it ends, so this check takes two minutes,

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.625
not an afternoon.

00:25:19.625 --> 00:25:23.538
Finally, if someone we haven't cleared asks for sensitive information,

00:25:23.538 --> 00:25:26.900
that's a flag to check, not a request to fulfil.

00:25:27.675 --> 00:25:29.238
Here's a situation.

00:25:29.238 --> 00:25:33.875
A long-time vendor asks for the Meridian board contact list to send 'a courtesy

00:25:33.875 --> 00:25:38.100
update.' Their NDA covers IT services only.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.350
What do you tell them, and what do you check?

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:46.362
Section 5 of 8: Command Hierarchy and Liaison.

00:25:46.362 --> 00:25:49.475
How the command hierarchy works across different tracks,

00:25:49.475 --> 00:25:53.125
and what it takes to serve as a liaison and point of contact.

00:25:55.025 --> 00:25:58.575
When something looks wrong, we don't fix it quietly on our own.

00:25:58.575 --> 00:26:01.338
Say a document went out without the right review.

00:26:01.338 --> 00:26:04.675
Ignoring it and fixing it ourselves are both risky.

00:26:05.475 --> 00:26:06.750
First, we notice.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.025
We catch the issue while it's still small.

00:26:09.025 --> 00:26:10.588
Next, we assess.

00:26:10.588 --> 00:26:13.562
How big is it, how urgent, and who else does it touch?

00:26:13.562 --> 00:26:18.225
Then, we escalate, to the right person through the proper channel, not around them.

00:26:18.225 --> 00:26:20.163
Finally, we inform.

00:26:20.163 --> 00:26:24.000
The executive should hear it from us before he hears it from anyone else.

00:26:24.775 --> 00:26:26.375
Here's a situation.

00:26:26.375 --> 00:26:31.488
At 4:30 p.m., you learn that a filing deadline for a Harlow matter document was

00:26:31.488 --> 00:26:32.875
missed at noon.

00:26:32.875 --> 00:26:37.112
Elias is in a meeting with the managing partner until 5:30.

00:26:37.112 --> 00:26:40.275
How do you make sure he hears it from you, and what do you say?

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:45.275
Who we report a problem to depends on which world it lives in:

00:26:45.275 --> 00:26:47.912
the office, the law firm or the household.

00:26:47.912 --> 00:26:49.612
Each has its own ladder.

00:26:49.612 --> 00:26:54.512
In a company: the CEO, the senior EA, the EA, the admin assistant.

00:26:54.512 --> 00:26:58.075
In a law firm: the managing partner, the senior legal assistant,

00:26:58.075 --> 00:27:00.350
the legal EA, the paralegal.

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:05.725
At home: the head of the family, the estate manager, the PA, the household staff.

00:27:06.525 --> 00:27:09.325
So first, we work out which world we're in.

00:27:09.325 --> 00:27:14.400
Next, in a company, we go up the business ladder, starting with our own executive.

00:27:16.325 --> 00:27:20.075
Then, in a law firm, we follow the stricter legal ladder,

00:27:20.075 --> 00:27:24.075
because an unauthorized decision there can become a legal problem.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:29.825
After that, at home, we go through the family's own ladder.

00:27:29.825 --> 00:27:32.825
Finally, if a problem sits in two worlds at once,

00:27:32.825 --> 00:27:36.825
like a household matter with legal risk, we raise it in both.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:39.262
Here's a situation.

00:27:39.262 --> 00:27:44.012
The Thornes' nanny says a man came to the door asking questions about Elias's work

00:27:44.012 --> 00:27:45.800
and took photos of the house.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.788
It's a household matter, but it might involve a case.

00:27:48.788 --> 00:27:52.175
Who do you tell, in which order, and through which chains?

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:57.387
Gatekeeping decides who reaches the executive.

00:27:57.387 --> 00:28:01.200
Being a liaison is about connecting people who need each other.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:06.375
A client needs something from Elias, a vendor needs something from staff,

00:28:06.375 --> 00:28:08.262
and it all flows through us.

00:28:08.262 --> 00:28:12.212
That only works if what comes out the other end is exactly what went in,

00:28:12.212 --> 00:28:14.850
and if people know we're the one to call.

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:20.725
First, we make sure we really understand what each side needs.

00:28:20.725 --> 00:28:23.975
Next, we carry the message accurately both ways,

00:28:23.975 --> 00:28:27.150
in words that make sense to the person receiving it.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:31.062
Then, we make it clear we're the one point of contact for this matter,

00:28:31.062 --> 00:28:33.325
so nobody's guessing who to ask.

00:28:35.275 --> 00:28:38.912
After that, we keep in mind which job we're doing at each moment:

00:28:38.912 --> 00:28:42.100
filtering access, or connecting two people.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:46.875
Finally, after the handoff, we check back with both sides to make sure it landed as

00:28:46.875 --> 00:28:48.000
intended.

00:28:48.775 --> 00:28:50.325
Here's a situation.

00:28:50.325 --> 00:28:55.362
You passed a vendor's question to Elias on Monday, and he answered on Tuesday.

00:28:55.362 --> 00:28:59.412
On Friday, the vendor calls asking why they never heard back.

00:28:59.412 --> 00:29:03.050
What part of the liaison loop was missed, and how do you fix it?

00:29:04.975 --> 00:29:08.375
A good liaison carries the message exactly as it was sent,

00:29:08.375 --> 00:29:11.150
in both directions, and does it quickly.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:16.487
Passing a request up, it's tempting to soften it or add our own spin.

00:29:16.487 --> 00:29:20.637
Passing his answer back, it's tempting to dress up a short reply so it sounds

00:29:20.637 --> 00:29:21.800
friendlier.

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:23.600
Both change the message.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:28.325
And a liaison so careful that everything slows down has failed just as much as one

00:29:28.325 --> 00:29:30.500
who lets everything straight through.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:34.937
So first, we quietly work out which world the request belongs to,

00:29:34.937 --> 00:29:38.362
office, legal or household, before we route it.

00:29:38.362 --> 00:29:40.300
That's often the real job.

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:47.400
After that, we watch our own speed, because a slow liaison becomes a bottleneck.

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:52.175
Finally, if we're not sure the message landed the way it was meant, we check back.

00:29:52.925 --> 00:29:54.387
Here's a situation.

00:29:54.387 --> 00:29:59.150
A partner sends Elias a long, polite email asking him to co-author a conference

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:00.062
paper.

00:30:00.062 --> 00:30:02.937
Elias's reply to you is: 'Not this year.

00:30:02.937 --> 00:30:07.575
Too much on.' Write the message you'd send back, accurate but professional.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.512
Section 6 of 8: Managing Up Basics.

00:30:12.512 --> 00:30:15.375
Bulletproof basics, the Three C's of managing up,

00:30:15.375 --> 00:30:18.175
and why credibility is earned, not claimed.

00:30:20.125 --> 00:30:24.025
Three everyday basics decide whether an executive trusts us:

00:30:24.025 --> 00:30:28.350
the inbox, the travel backup plan and the full life of a meeting.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:32.162
None of this is glamorous, and that's the point.

00:30:32.162 --> 00:30:36.712
An executive judges us on whether the ordinary things always go right.

00:30:36.712 --> 00:30:40.500
A cancelled flight should be an inconvenience, not a crisis.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:43.700
And meetings don't end when everyone leaves the room.

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:48.862
First, as each email arrives, we decide straight away:

00:30:48.862 --> 00:30:51.775
action, information only, or someone else's.

00:30:51.775 --> 00:30:55.450
Next, where it makes sense, we draft the reply in the executive's voice,

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:57.788
so all he has to do is press send.

00:30:57.788 --> 00:31:00.850
Then, when we book travel, we line up the backup too.

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:05.387
If the 2 p.m. flight is cancelled, the 4 p.m. is already on hold.

00:31:05.387 --> 00:31:08.050
After that, we set the agenda before the meeting;

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:11.550
that's how we go from taking notes to shaping the result.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:15.950
Finally, we follow every action item through until it's actually done.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:19.062
Pick the basic you're weakest on today.

00:31:19.062 --> 00:31:23.175
What's one real habit, not an intention, that would fix it this week?

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:28.137
Every update we send gets judged on three things:

00:31:28.137 --> 00:31:31.850
is it clear, is it consistent, and can he rely on it?

00:31:32.625 --> 00:31:36.962
Clarity means he never has to guess what we're telling him or what we need.

00:31:36.962 --> 00:31:42.625
Consistency means we do things the same way every time, so he knows what to expect.

00:31:42.625 --> 00:31:46.237
And credibility means that when we say something, it's true.

00:31:46.237 --> 00:31:47.900
That one matters most.

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:53.400
So first, before sending an update, we check it's clear.

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:56.800
Next, we check it's consistent: our usual approach,

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.612
not something improvised because today's busy.

00:31:59.612 --> 00:32:04.662
Then, if speed and reliability pull against each other, reliability wins.

00:32:04.662 --> 00:32:09.100
A quick answer that's wrong is worse than a slightly slower one that's right.

00:32:11.025 --> 00:32:14.275
After that, when we're under pressure and clarity starts slipping,

00:32:14.275 --> 00:32:18.250
we slow down and restate the main point before we hit send.

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:22.375
Finally, every so often we reread our own recent messages,

00:32:22.375 --> 00:32:25.550
because these habits fade quietly when we're busy.

00:32:26.325 --> 00:32:27.487
Try this one.

00:32:27.487 --> 00:32:32.437
Elias asks, 'Is the Meridian binder at the courthouse?' You think the courier picked

00:32:32.437 --> 00:32:34.587
it up, but you haven't confirmed.

00:32:34.587 --> 00:32:37.450
What's the reply that protects your credibility?

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:41.612
We can't announce that we're credible.

00:32:41.612 --> 00:32:45.975
The executive gives us credibility, one reliable week at a time,

00:32:45.975 --> 00:32:49.000
and it comes from a few things done over and over.

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:55.137
First, reliability: accurate, finished and on time, every time.

00:32:55.137 --> 00:32:57.050
Next, the no-surprises rule.

00:32:57.050 --> 00:33:00.737
If we know about something, he knows about it too, even if it seems minor.

00:33:00.737 --> 00:33:03.150
Then, we treat small decisions as if they might matter,

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:06.737
because some quietly carry money, legal or reputation risk.

00:33:06.737 --> 00:33:10.412
After that, discretion is our default, especially around investors,

00:33:10.412 --> 00:33:12.850
legal matters, family and deals.

00:33:12.850 --> 00:33:16.600
Finally, if we ever damage that trust, we rebuild it steadily.

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:18.975
One good week doesn't undo a bad one.

00:33:19.725 --> 00:33:21.400
Here's a situation.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.875
Last month you sent a partner the wrong version of a contract,

00:33:24.875 --> 00:33:28.187
and now Elias double-checks everything you send.

00:33:28.187 --> 00:33:31.875
What does rebuilding his trust look like over the next few weeks?

00:33:33.775 --> 00:33:34.525
Quick check.

00:33:34.525 --> 00:33:39.675
Does the 'No-Surprises Rule' mean A, surprises are fine as long as they're positive?

00:33:39.675 --> 00:33:43.062
B, only bad news needs to be flagged early?

00:33:43.062 --> 00:33:47.275
C, an executive should never be blindsided by something their assistant already

00:33:47.275 --> 00:33:48.037
knew?

00:33:48.037 --> 00:33:51.825
Or D, executives enjoy occasional surprises?

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:53.925
Take a second.

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:55.200
It's C.

00:33:55.087 --> 00:33:59.387
Anything the assistant already knows that could affect the executive needs to reach

00:33:59.387 --> 00:34:02.300
them proactively, good or bad.

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:08.250
Section 7 of 8: Client Profiling and the Dossier.

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:12.175
Building a client profile, creating a comprehensive dossier,

00:34:12.175 --> 00:34:14.775
and setting up the trackers that put it to work.

00:34:16.675 --> 00:34:20.950
A client profile is something we build once, so we never have to ask the same

00:34:20.950 --> 00:34:22.387
question twice.

00:34:22.387 --> 00:34:26.962
Being asked, for the third time, whether you like morning or afternoon meetings

00:34:26.962 --> 00:34:28.850
wears down trust fast.

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:34.587
First, the basics: role, organization and how far their authority reaches.

00:34:34.587 --> 00:34:36.675
Next, how they like to communicate.

00:34:36.675 --> 00:34:38.225
Blunt or detailed?

00:34:38.225 --> 00:34:39.712
Written or spoken?

00:34:39.712 --> 00:34:43.625
Then, their meeting rules, like breaks between meetings and off-limits times,

00:34:43.625 --> 00:34:46.512
because those are the rules new people break most.

00:34:46.512 --> 00:34:50.850
After that, travel preferences, in enough detail to book without asking.

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:54.475
Finally, any quirks, in plain, respectful language.

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:56.887
Here's a situation.

00:34:56.887 --> 00:35:01.100
A new colleague books Elias an early-morning red-eye before a court day,

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:03.600
with three back-to-back meetings after it.

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:06.875
Which parts of his profile would have stopped both mistakes?

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:11.350
A dossier goes deeper than a profile.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:15.600
If the profile is a quick reference card, the dossier is the full handbook,

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:20.700
in four parts: the professional picture, the personal details that actually help,

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:23.500
his standing instructions, and his quirks.

00:35:24.250 --> 00:35:29.325
The test is simple: could someone who has never met Elias run his account from it?

00:35:31.275 --> 00:35:35.675
First, the firm and his role, so the reader understands the professional setting.

00:35:35.675 --> 00:35:41.075
Next, family and personal details, but only what genuinely helps us support him.

00:35:41.075 --> 00:35:45.312
Then, his standing instructions, as clear rules anyone could follow without

00:35:45.312 --> 00:35:46.462
interpreting.

00:35:46.462 --> 00:35:50.200
After that, his quirks, in plain words, never judgment.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:53.450
Finally, we run that test before we call it done.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:55.762
Here's a situation.

00:35:55.762 --> 00:36:00.500
A substitute books Elias a window seat in row 22 for a London flight,

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:05.762
because the dossier said 'prefers comfortable seats.' Rewrite that entry so it can't

00:36:05.762 --> 00:36:07.000
be misread.

00:36:08.925 --> 00:36:12.075
Every fact in a dossier needs its reason attached,

00:36:12.075 --> 00:36:15.800
because a rule without a reason is the one that gets broken.

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:20.287
Take Elias's fifteen-minute debrief after key sessions.

00:36:20.287 --> 00:36:23.212
Listed on its own, it can look optional.

00:36:23.212 --> 00:36:27.737
Explain that he needs that time to process a court session before his next call,

00:36:27.737 --> 00:36:29.425
and nobody will touch it.

00:36:30.225 --> 00:36:32.950
So first, we put each fact under the right heading:

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:37.325
firm and role, family, standing instructions or quirks.

00:36:37.325 --> 00:36:41.000
Then, we ask what would go wrong if someone got this entry wrong.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:43.475
That tells us how carefully to word it.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:50.550
After that, we write for someone who's never met him, with no inside shorthand.

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:55.625
Finally, we compare our entries with the Elias examples to make sure ours are just

00:36:55.625 --> 00:36:56.925
as specific.

00:36:57.700 --> 00:36:59.237
Here's a situation.

00:36:59.237 --> 00:37:04.312
Elias sends his 5:30 a.m. voice notes and expects an agenda by 8.

00:37:04.312 --> 00:37:09.250
A dossier entry says only: 'Voice notes early.' What would a new assistant get

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:11.525
wrong, and what should the entry say?

00:37:13.425 --> 00:37:15.662
The dossier is the reference book.

00:37:15.662 --> 00:37:21.050
Trackers are the everyday tools that put it into practice, updated as things happen.

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:25.937
We start with just three: travel, inbox and meeting rhythms.

00:37:25.937 --> 00:37:29.300
Three we'll actually keep up beat ten we'll let go stale.

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:33.812
First, the travel tracker holds the details that change a booking:

00:37:33.812 --> 00:37:37.675
seat, connections and whether we rebook without asking.

00:37:39.650 --> 00:37:43.925
Next, the inbox tracker records the exact format he expects,

00:37:43.925 --> 00:37:47.125
like one-line summaries with the details attached.

00:37:49.075 --> 00:37:53.787
Then, the meetings tracker holds hard rules, such as no back-to-back sessions and

00:37:53.787 --> 00:37:55.250
the breaks he needs.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:59.350
Finally, we update every tracker the moment we learn something new.

00:38:00.125 --> 00:38:01.362
Here's a test.

00:38:01.362 --> 00:38:04.675
Elias's London flight is delayed three hours.

00:38:04.675 --> 00:38:07.562
His travel tracker says aisle seat near the front,

00:38:07.562 --> 00:38:10.650
no connections, and rebook without being asked.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:13.025
What do you do before he even notices?

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:18.125
We use the same client for all ten days on purpose,

00:38:18.125 --> 00:38:21.575
because in a real job, what you learn keeps building.

00:38:22.400 --> 00:38:26.225
Most training brings a brand-new person for every exercise.

00:38:26.225 --> 00:38:28.112
Real life isn't like that.

00:38:28.112 --> 00:38:30.737
You work for the same executive for months.

00:38:30.737 --> 00:38:34.575
So what we learn about Elias today will come back in the calendar work,

00:38:34.575 --> 00:38:37.800
the inbox work and the travel planning later on.

00:38:39.750 --> 00:38:43.850
First, we treat every fact about Elias as something we'll need again.

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:47.600
Next, when a later exercise leans on something from before,

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:50.250
we try to remember it before looking it up.

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:52.575
That recall is part of the training.

00:38:52.575 --> 00:38:56.175
Then, if something seems to contradict what we know about him,

00:38:56.175 --> 00:39:00.025
we raise it with the trainer instead of quietly working around it.

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:06.225
After that, we let the repetition help us; it's what makes things stick.

00:39:06.225 --> 00:39:10.262
Finally, by the last day, we should be able to describe his rules,

00:39:10.262 --> 00:39:13.325
family and communication style from memory.

00:39:14.075 --> 00:39:15.600
Here's a situation.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:19.912
On Day 3, a travel scenario says Elias prefers window seats.

00:39:19.912 --> 00:39:22.950
Your Day 1 dossier says aisle seats only.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:25.825
What do you do, and why does it matter to raise it?

00:39:27.775 --> 00:39:31.225
Section 8 of 8: Written Communication Frameworks.

00:39:31.225 --> 00:39:34.800
The ACT email framework, and BLUF, bottom line up front,

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.550
in emails, updates and voice notes.

00:39:39.425 --> 00:39:44.050
When an executive sends a messy request, a clear reply turns it into something you

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:45.600
can both act on.

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.437
Elias thinks fast and talks in fragments.

00:39:49.437 --> 00:39:53.612
A 5:30 a.m. voice note might have three tasks tangled together.

00:39:53.612 --> 00:39:58.187
Reply 'Got it!', and we find out three days later that we misunderstood.

00:39:58.187 --> 00:40:00.150
ACT fixes that.

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:04.625
First, we acknowledge: we restate the request in our own words,

00:40:04.625 --> 00:40:07.800
so any misunderstanding shows up right away.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:11.800
Next, we clarify: we ask only what we can't work out ourselves,

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:13.875
and we check the dossier first.

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:19.775
Then, we give a timeline: what will happen, by when and who owns it.

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:24.725
After that, we keep it short, answer first.

00:40:24.725 --> 00:40:28.325
Finally, we send it quickly, right after the request arrives,

00:40:28.325 --> 00:40:30.525
not as a summary hours later.

00:40:31.325 --> 00:40:32.837
Try drafting one.

00:40:32.837 --> 00:40:36.225
Elias's voice note says: 'Move the Meridian call,

00:40:36.225 --> 00:40:39.900
get me something on the Singapore thing, and find out why the retainer invoice

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:43.100
hasn't gone out.' What's your ACT reply?

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:49.250
If Elias reads only your first line, he should already know what you need and by

00:40:49.250 --> 00:40:50.125
when.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:53.362
BLUF stands for Bottom Line Up Front.

00:40:53.362 --> 00:40:57.862
The decision, the answer or the action goes in the very first sentence;

00:40:57.862 --> 00:41:01.800
everything after it is detail he can stop reading at any time.

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:05.775
It feels backwards, because most of us were taught to explain first.

00:41:05.775 --> 00:41:08.950
But busy people often only read the first line.

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:12.550
First, before we write, we finish this sentence:

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:15.800
'The one thing Elias needs to know or decide is...'.

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:17.575
That becomes our first line.

00:41:17.575 --> 00:41:21.525
Next, we label the ask: 'Decision needed', 'FYI,

00:41:21.525 --> 00:41:24.775
no action', or 'Approval by 3 p.m.'.

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:30.175
Then, we add only the details he needs to act: dates,

00:41:30.175 --> 00:41:33.500
amounts, names and options, in a few short lines.

00:41:35.425 --> 00:41:40.175
After that, we move the background below a clear break or into an attachment.

00:41:40.175 --> 00:41:43.825
Finally, we finish with the exact next step and deadline,

00:41:43.825 --> 00:41:46.825
then reread just the first line the way he would.

00:41:47.625 --> 00:41:49.050
Here's a situation.

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:53.187
An update to Elias begins: 'Hi Elias, hope your trip was good!

00:41:53.187 --> 00:41:57.387
So yesterday I spoke to the courier, then the clerk…' The question he needs to

00:41:57.387 --> 00:41:59.650
answer is in the fourth paragraph.

00:41:59.650 --> 00:42:01.750
Rewrite just the first line.

00:42:03.700 --> 00:42:07.687
Bottom line first works everywhere: emails, chat messages,

00:42:07.687 --> 00:42:10.825
status updates, voice notes and phone calls.

00:42:10.825 --> 00:42:16.037
On a call, it sounds like 'Quick one: I need a yes or no on the retainer.' On a

00:42:16.037 --> 00:42:20.150
phone screen, the first eighty characters should tell the whole story.

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:24.175
First, the subject or first line carries the bottom line,

00:42:24.175 --> 00:42:29.125
with a label like Decision, FYI, Action Taken or At Risk.

00:42:29.125 --> 00:42:34.400
Next, one line on why it matters today, usually the deadline or the consequence.

00:42:36.325 --> 00:42:40.350
Then, we give only the details needed to act, in short points.

00:42:42.275 --> 00:42:45.350
After that, we close with the next step and who owns it,

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.400
and attach the background instead of pasting it in.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:53.575
Finally, we check the preview: would the first line alone make sense on a phone?

00:42:54.375 --> 00:42:56.100
One last situation.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:43:01.850
It's 2 p.m., and a filing due at 5 p.m. is at risk because the courier is delayed;

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:03.725
your backup is e-filing.

00:43:03.725 --> 00:43:08.075
Write the subject line and first line of the status update to Elias.

00:43:10.050 --> 00:43:11.587
Now it's your turn.

00:43:11.587 --> 00:43:14.475
In the Client Dossier and Gatekeeping Practice,

00:43:14.475 --> 00:43:18.650
you'll build a comprehensive client dossier and preference trackers for Elias

00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:22.500
Thorne, respond to a real ACT-framework voice note,

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:25.925
then practice inbound gatekeeping under pressure.

